we're talking about a segment of the  Beltway around Washington DC where it  should take you 23 minutes to go along  that 10 or 15 mile route it takes you  nearly an hour and a half the three  steps toward improving mobility is  certainly increase in capacity that's  one as a base of principle the second is  to really look at ways of allocating  that capital that that investment in a  way that's efficient and productive and  that's using market-oriented thinking  that includes at this point we'd have to  use cost-benefit analysis in that type  of thing but the third principle is by  using comprehensive pricing really  overlay a market-oriented framework on  top of physical infrastructure so in  terms of the future in terms of how  we're going to decide what roads are put  where in that it really can rely and  should rely on pricing this is not  without precedent historically trolley  lines were extended out to new areas to  facilitate urban development and those  were fully in many cases they were fully  paid for by the private sector by the  land developers so it's really a  question of updating a principle that  got lost in the 20th century and using  modern technology with all the  efficiencies that have come with that as  well as a good market oriented framework  to thinking about transportation that  allows us to update it and really  provide for efficient investments but  the bottom line is that if you're not  willing to pay for the investments in  infrastructure that infrastructure  probably should not be invested so all  three things have to come together in  order for us to make a meaningful impact  on mobility in the United States  you
 

